  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 128 PM MDT Tue Oct 13 2026\n\nVALLEY RAIN AND MOUNTAIN SNOW THROUGH LATE WEDNESDAY:\n\nA low pressure system will bring widespread precipitation to \nour area through late Wednesday night, including valley rain and\nmountain snow. Water vapor imagery shows this low is currently \ncentered over southern Nevada, and is allowing for southwesterly\nflow into our region today. Moisture advection in this flow is \nkeeping precipitable water around 150 to 200% of normal for \nthis time of the year, with the most anomalous atmospheric \nmoisture favoring Colorado's higher terrain. Today the low will \nmove eastward, bringing increased lift as the associated \nvorticity begins to advect into our CWA. Thus, the ongoing \nshower activity will increase in coverage through the day. A few  \nembedded thunderstorms are possible during the afternoon today \nand tomorrow as well. \n\nTraffic cameras are presently showing rain, but in higher\nelevation areas rain is expected to transition to snow or a\nrain/snow mix tonight as temperatures cool. The center of the\nlow pressure system will move across western Colorado during  \nthe day Wednesday, providing substantial lift as it does so. \nPeak snowfall rates in the southern mountains are forecast to \noccur between 6 AM and 12 PM Wednesday, although snowfall could \npersist through Wednesday night. In the central and northern \nColorado mountains snowfall rates should increase Wednesday \nmorning, but peak rates are expected between noon and midnight. \nSnow levels look to drop to about 7000 ft Wednesday morning, \nbut relatively warm road conditions leading up to this event \nintroduces uncertainty about how much snow will accumulate, \nespecially as temperatures warm and snow levels rise during the \nday. Melting and wet snow could result in slushy or icy road \nconditions so be sure to use caution if traveling through areas \nwith snow, especially over mountain passes. Elevation based \nWinter Weather Advisories have been issued for the San Juan \nMountains and for Colorado's northern and central mountains \nbeginning 6 AM Wednesday. \n\nDRIER AND WARMER IN THE LATTER HALF OF THE WEEK:\n\nDrier air moves into our region after the low pressure system\nmoves east of the Rocky Mountains. The current forecast keeps\nprecipitation chances low Thursday afternoon through Sunday,\nespecially outside of the higher terrain areas. High\ntemperatures are expected to remain below normal through Sunday,\nbut should increase by 5 or 10 degrees from today's highs by \nthe weekend.",
